						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="grid-col-6 desktop:grid-col-3 padding-top-2 padding-bottom-2 padding-left-3 padding-right-1 desktop:padding-3 desktop:padding-left-0">
						<div class="padding-0 display-flex flex-align-end flex-justify-end">
							<div class="hds-cover-wrapper hds-page-intro-image-2">
							<figure class="hds-media-background  "><img decoding="async" width="985" height="739" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/acero-uasp.jpeg?w=985" class="attachment-large size-large" alt="NASA researchers test a prototype mobile air traffic kit for unmanned aircraft systems in Redding, California." style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 55% 49%; object-position: 55% 49%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/acero-uasp.jpeg 985w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/acero-uasp.jpeg?resize=300,225 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/acero-uasp.jpeg?resize=768,576 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/acero-uasp.jpeg?resize=400,300 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/acero-uasp.jpeg?resize=600,450 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/acero-uasp.jpeg?resize=900,675 900w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 985px) 100vw, 985px" /></figure>							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="grid-col-6 desktop:grid-col-3 padding-top-2 padding-bottom-2 padding-left-1 padding-right-3 desktop:padding-3 desktop:padding-left-0">
						<div class="padding-0 display-flex flex-align-end flex-justify-end">
							<div class="hds-cover-wrapper hds-page-intro-image-3">
							<figure class="hds-media-background  "><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="674" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?w=1024" class="attachment-large size-large" alt="NASA researchers participating in a High Density Vertiplex (HDV) study in the Airspace Operations Laboratory (AOL)." style="transform: scale(1.2); transform-origin: 38% 56%; object-position: 38% 56%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg 1421w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?resize=300,197 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?resize=768,505 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?resize=1024,674 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?resize=400,263 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?resize=600,395 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?resize=900,592 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/hdv-aval-sim.jpg?resize=1200,790 1200w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
									</div>
				<div class="grid-row grid-container grid-container-block padding-top-0 padding-bottom-8">
						<h1 class="page-heading-md display-block width-full margin-bottom-1">Human Systems Integration Division</h1>
					<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-7 padding-right-0 desktop:padding-right-6 padding-bottom-5 desktop:padding-bottom-0">
						<div class="margin-bottom-2">
							<p class="p-lg margin-0 padding-0">Humans are arguably the most critical element in the safety, reliability, and performance of complex systems. Our highly adaptive problem-solving capabilities create resilient operations across aerospace applications, especially with the advent of increasingly intelligent software and hardware systems. Advances in machine learning, adaptive automation, advanced display technologies, and information accessibility create new challenges for human performance and new opportunities for human-automation teaming.<br><br>In aeronautics, humans are the backbone of a national aviation system that must handle growing consumer demands. In space, long-duration exploration missions will require revolutionary changes in the roles of the astronauts and mission controllers to support autonomous operations. For both aeronautics and space, the design of hardware and software systems must address the need for safe, efficient and cost-effective operations, both in-flight and on the ground.</p>
						</div>
						<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/division-overview" target="_self" class="button-primary link-external-false">
							<span class="section-heading-md">
                                Division Overview <span class="usa-sr-only">about Human Systems Integration Division</span>                            </span>
							<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
						</a>
					</div>
								<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-5 padding-left-0 desktop:padding-left-6">
					<div class="grid-row margin-bottom-3">
						<div class="grid-col border-top-1px border-color-carbon-30-important padding-top-2 margin-right-1">
							<h2 class="label margin-0 color-carbon-50-important">Location</h2>
							<p class="p-lg font-weight-bold margin-0 padding-0">NASA Ames Research Center, Moffett Field, CA</p>
						</div>
						<div class="grid-col border-top-1px border-color-carbon-30-important padding-top-2 margin-left-1">
							<h2 class="label margin-0 color-carbon-50-important">Founded</h2>
							<p class="p-lg font-weight-bold margin-0 padding-0">January, 1989</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="grid-row">
						<div class="grid-col border-top-1px border-color-carbon-30-important padding-top-2 margin-right-1">
							<h2 class="label margin-0 color-carbon-50-important">People</h2>
							<p class="p-lg font-weight-bold margin-0 padding-0">130+</p>
						</div>
						<div class="grid-col border-top-1px border-color-carbon-30-important padding-top-2 margin-left-1">
							<h2 class="label margin-0 color-carbon-50-important">division chief</h2>
							<p class="p-lg font-weight-bold margin-0 padding-0">Jessica Nowinski</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
								</div>
		</div>
		</div>
	</div>

<div id="" class="hds-card-grid padding-top-7 padding-bottom-3 padding-x-0 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-card-grid">			<div class="hds-card-grid-header grid-container grid-container-block">
				<div class="grid-row flex-align-start margin-bottom-5">
					<div class="desktop:grid-col-8 margin-bottom-2 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
						<div class="margin-bottom-1">
							<h2 class="section-heading-sm line-height-sm">Research Branches</h2>
						</div>
													<p class="margin-0 padding-0">The Human Systems Integration Division is composed of three research branches. </p>
											</div>
					<div class="desktop:grid-col display-flex flex-align-center desktop:flex-justify-end">
											</div>
				</div>
				<div class="grid-row grid-gap-2"></div>
			</div>
			<div class="hds-card-grid-cards grid-container grid-container-block">
				<div class="hds-cards-included grid-row">
					<div class="hds-card-custom hds-card color-carbon-black display-flex flex-column margin-bottom-5 mobile:grid-col-12 tablet:grid-col-6 desktop:grid-col-4 margin-bottom-6"><div class="hds-card-inner"><div class="hds-card-thumbnail hds-card-hover-zoom-image margin-bottom-2 bg-carbon-black"><figure class="hds-media-background  "><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="713" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?w=1024" class="attachment-large size-large" alt="NASA Artemis 1 Launch Director, Charlie Blackwell-Thompson using NASA MAS software during the Artemis 1 mission." style="transform: scale(1.2); transform-origin: 54% 66%; object-position: 54% 66%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png 1489w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?resize=300,209 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?resize=768,535 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?resize=1024,713 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?resize=400,279 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?resize=600,418 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?resize=900,627 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/mas-artemis-1-launch.png?resize=1200,836 1200w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure></div><div class="padding-right-0"><div class="margin-bottom-1"><h3 class="heading-18">Human-Computer Interaction</h3></div><p class="margin-top-0 line-height-lg">The Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) Group contributes to the development of measurably better NASA software through careful application of user-centered research and design methods. </p><div class="margin-top-2"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-computer-interaction" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md link-external-0"><span role="textbox" aria-multiline="true" aria-label="Write button text here" class="line-height-alt-1" style="white-space: pre-wrap; min-width: 1px;">Branch Overview</span><svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg></a></div></div></div></div><div class="hds-card-custom hds-card color-carbon-black display-flex flex-column margin-bottom-5 mobile:grid-col-12 tablet:grid-col-6 desktop:grid-col-4 margin-bottom-6"><div class="hds-card-inner"><div class="hds-card-thumbnail hds-card-hover-zoom-image margin-bottom-2 bg-carbon-black"><figure class="hds-media-background  "><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="768" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?w=1024" class="attachment-large size-large" alt="NASA researcher Dr. Erin-Flynn Evans prepping a research subject for a NASA Human Fatigue Countermeasures Study." style="transform: scale(1.2); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg 1452w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?resize=300,225 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?resize=768,576 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?resize=1024,768 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?resize=400,300 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?resize=600,450 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?resize=900,675 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/fatigue-study-7.jpg?resize=1200,900 1200w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure></div><div class="padding-right-0"><div class="margin-bottom-1"><h3 class="heading-18">Human Performance</h3></div><p class="margin-top-0 line-height-lg">The Human Performance Group performs research and technology development to enhance health, productivity, and safety in aerospace environments. </p><div class="margin-top-2"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-performance" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md link-external-0"><span role="textbox" aria-multiline="true" aria-label="Write button text here" class="line-height-alt-1" style="white-space: pre-wrap; min-width: 1px;">Branch Overview</span><svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg></a></div></div></div></div><div class="hds-card-custom hds-card color-carbon-black display-flex flex-column margin-bottom-5 mobile:grid-col-12 tablet:grid-col-6 desktop:grid-col-4 margin-bottom-6"><div class="hds-card-inner"><div class="hds-card-thumbnail hds-card-hover-zoom-image margin-bottom-2 bg-carbon-black"><figure class="hds-media-background  "><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="768" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?w=1024" class="attachment-large size-large" alt="NASA researchers during a Upper Class E Traffic Management (ETM) research simulation." style="transform: scale(1.2); transform-origin: 47% 65%; object-position: 47% 65%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg 1600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=300,225 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=768,576 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=1024,768 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=1536,1152 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=400,300 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=600,450 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=900,675 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/img-0158-web.jpg?resize=1200,900 1200w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure></div><div class="padding-right-0"><div class="margin-bottom-1"><h3 class="heading-18">Integration and Evaluation</h3></div><p class="margin-top-0 line-height-lg">The Integration and Evaluation Group is committed to improving the efficiency and safety of the Air Transportation System, with emphasis on emerging operations enabled by increased automation. </p><div class="margin-top-2"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/integration-and-evaluation" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md link-external-0"><span role="textbox" aria-multiline="true" aria-label="Write button text here" class="line-height-alt-1" style="white-space: pre-wrap; min-width: 1px;">Branch Overview</span><svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg></a></div></div></div></div>				</div>
			</div>
	</div>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading alignwide has-text-align-left">Latest News</h2>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">Aviation Safety</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">NASA&#039;s Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS) Celebrates 50 Years</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">5/21/26</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">April 15<sup>th</sup>, 2026 marked the 50th anniversary of the NASA Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS) operated by NASA Ames Research Center since 1976. In that time, ASRS has received more than 2.3 million voluntary, confidential reports describing safety issues, near misses, and close calls from aviation professionals across the national Airspace System (NAS) including pilots, air traffic controllers, dispatchers, cabin crew, maintenance technicians, ground operations, and drone operators.<br> <br>ASRS receives more than 350 reports a day. A small team analyzes each one and works to rapidly share urgent safety information with the aviation community. ASRS highlights emerging issues, shares best practices, and thereby helps prevent future accidents through a variety of communication products including safety alerts, monthly teleconferences, newsletters, analyses, and a public database of reported aviation incidents.<br> <br>ASRS data informs research and development efforts across NASA and government agencies including the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) and the National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB). Its insights have contributed to decades of human factors research on a vast array of topics such as Crew Resource Management, Pilot-ATC communication, checklist design, pilot decision making, fatigue management, low visibility landing and surface operations, automation management, and the integration of Unmanned Aircraft Systems in the NAS.<br> <br>ASRS data also support FAA regulation, policy, procedures, and initiatives such as airspace classification, runway incursion mitigation, and Notice to Air Missions (NOTAM) modernization. The NTSB values the insights gleaned from ASRS reports to inform accident investigations and develop mitigations to prevent future accidents. ASRS data have been used to monitor the safety impacts of emerging risks such as COVID-19, 5G Interference, and commercial space launches.</p>
				<a href="https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md" rel="noopener">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about NASA&#039;s Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS) Celebrates 50 Years</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="899" height="629" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/screenshot-2026-05-21-at-12-47-12-pm.png?w=899" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="Screenshot of the NASA Aviation Safety and Reporting System (ASRS) website." style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 40% 0%; object-position: 40% 0%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/screenshot-2026-05-21-at-12-47-12-pm.png 899w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/screenshot-2026-05-21-at-12-47-12-pm.png?resize=300,210 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/screenshot-2026-05-21-at-12-47-12-pm.png?resize=768,537 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/screenshot-2026-05-21-at-12-47-12-pm.png?resize=400,280 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/screenshot-2026-05-21-at-12-47-12-pm.png?resize=600,420 600w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 899px) 100vw, 899px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">Screenshot of the NASA Aviation Safety and Reporting System (ASRS) website.</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>


<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">Artemis II Mission</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">The Human Systems Integration Division Provides Multiple Contributions to the Artemis II Mission and Launch</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">4/8/26</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">The Human Systems Integration Division at NASA Ames Research Center has been heavily involved in multiple aspects of NASA’s <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/mission/artemis-ii/">Artemis II</a> mission and launch, contributing critical software support to mission operations personnel, safety support via fatigue countermeasures and ongoing on-site observational research during the mission. <br> <br>The <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-computer-interaction/cross-program-integrated-data-system/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-computer-interaction/cross-program-integrated-data-system/">Cross-Program Integrated Data System (CPIDS)</a> team, part of the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-computer-interaction/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-computer-interaction/">Human-Computer Interaction Group (HCI)</a>, developed a collection of software solutions that provided real-time support for Artemis II launch operations and mission support personnel. Two CPIDS applications, the Launch Commit Criteria Management System (CP-LMS) and the Cross-Program Operations and Maintenance Requirements and Specifications Management System (CP-OMS), contributed to a successful launch by allowing launch support personnel at Kennedy Space Center to quickly monitor and troubleshoot any pre-launch anomalies. Several other CPIDS tools will continue to be used throughout the duration of the Artemis II mission to track in-flight anomalies, identify items for investigation, and provide immediate access to schematics of the Orion capsule.<br> <br>Of course, these tools will provide the most benefit when their human operators are able to perform at an optimal level. In the lead-up to the Artemis II launch, the Human Systems Integration Division’s <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-performance/fatigue-countermeasures-laboratory/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-performance/fatigue-countermeasures-laboratory/">Fatigue Countermeasures Laboratory</a> provided guidance to mission personnel and the Artemis II astronauts around sleep schedules and other circadian rhythm-related issues which may affect their performance during the complex and strenuous 10-day mission.<br> <br>Finally, to study and collect as much data as possible from the Artemis II mission, several Human Systems Integration Division researchers were deployed to Johnson Space Center to conduct observational research on anomaly recording, data analysis, and real-time decision-making. These observations will assist with further refinement and improvement of the Division’s support for future NASA missions.</p>
				<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/mission/artemis-ii/" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about The Human Systems Integration Division Provides Multiple Contributions to the Artemis II Mission and Launch</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="1536" height="824" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?w=1536" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="Jeff Spaulding, NASA Senior Test Director for the Artemis II mission, using the CP-LMS (Launch Commit Criteria Management System) application at Kennedy Space Center before the launch of Artemis II" style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 57% 83%; object-position: 57% 83%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png 3024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=300,161 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=768,412 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=1024,549 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=1536,824 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=2048,1098 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=400,215 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=600,322 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=900,483 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=1200,644 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/lms-during-a2-2.png?resize=2000,1073 2000w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">Jeff Spaulding, NASA Senior Test Director for the Artemis II mission, using the CP-LMS (Launch Commit Criteria Management System) application at Kennedy Space Center before the launch of Artemis II</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>


<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">Air Traffic Management/Wildfire Response</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">Airspace Operations Laboratory Researchers Present and Moderate Discussions at the ACERO Workshop</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">1/29/26</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">Members of the Human Systems Integration Division’s Airspace Operation Laboratory (AOL) presented research materials and moderated knowledge elicitation sessions at the ACERO Post-TCL1 Technology Transfer and UTM BVLOS Workshop on January 27, 2026.  The workshop brought together NASA, the <a href="https://www.faa.gov/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.faa.gov/" rel="noopener">Federal Aviation Administration (FAA)</a>, industry partners, and public safety stakeholders for a full day of in-person activities at <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/ames/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/ames/">NASA Ames Research Center</a>.  <br> <br><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/armd/aosp/acero-wildfire/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/armd/aosp/acero-wildfire/">ACERO</a> team members had an in-depth exchange on research outcomes and operational pathways following the completion of Technical Capability Level 1 (TCL1). The workshop opened with program-level context on the TCL framework and ACERO objectives, followed by detailed briefings on the Portable Airspace Management System (PAMS) system, including flight test results, and performance analysis. <br> <br><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/people/lynne-martin/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/people/lynne-martin/">Dr. Lynne Martin</a> was on hand to present details of the human factors research that they have been conducting in the field with wildland fire response teams from multiple agencies. Afternoon presentations framed Unmanned Aircraft System Traffic Management Beyond Visual Line of Sight (UTM BVLOS) operations within the broader UTM ecosystem, covering public safety integration, key sites, and emerging regulatory pathways. The interactive breakout sessions included focused discussion on topics such as strategic deconfliction, conformance monitoring, and operational priority. The workshop concluded with synthesis of breakout findings, open discussion of upcoming opportunities, and closing remarks reinforcing collaboration toward safe, scalable BVLOS and emergency response operations.</p>
				<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/armd/aosp/acero-wildfire/" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about Airspace Operations Laboratory Researchers Present and Moderate Discussions at the ACERO Workshop</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="1167" height="782" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg?w=1167" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="Dr. Lynne Martin, NASA researcher at NASA Ames Research Center&#039;s Human Systems Integration Division, is shown here presenting at the ACERO Post-TCL1 Technology Transfer and UTM BVLOS Workshop on January 27, 2026." style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 57% 83%; object-position: 57% 83%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg 1167w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg?resize=300,201 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg?resize=768,515 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg?resize=1024,686 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg?resize=400,268 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg?resize=600,402 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/acero-wkshp-martin.jpg?resize=900,603 900w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1167px) 100vw, 1167px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">Dr. Lynne Martin, NASA researcher at NASA Ames Research Center&#8217;s Human Systems Integration Division, is shown here presenting at the ACERO Post-TCL1 Technology Transfer and UTM BVLOS Workshop on January 27, 2026.</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
