<h2 class="wp-block-heading alignwide">Featured Research and Projects</h2>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">SIMULATORS</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">ACELeRATE</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">Aerospace Cognitive Engineering Lab Rapid Automation Test Environment (ACELeRATE)</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">A new generation of aerospace innovators are looking for ways to quickly and efficiently transport people in a safe and environmentally friendly manner. In the not-too-distant future, passengers and goods are expected to routinely fly aboard a new breed of cleaner, smarter air vehicles. This represents a new and significant challenge to the <a href="https://www.faa.gov" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.faa.gov" rel="noopener">Federal Aviation Agency (FAA)</a> which is responsible for aircraft certification, pilot licensing, operating approval and airspace integration. Aerospace Cognitive Engineering Lab Rapid Automation Test (ACELeRATE) simulator. ACELeRATE is an adaptable fixed-base aircraft simulator focused on the investigation of the performance and interaction of pilots and increasingly  automated aircraft systems.</p>
				<a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20205006164/downloads/ACELeRATE%20Sim%20for%20eVTOL.pdf" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md" rel="noopener">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about ACELeRATE</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="1041" height="693" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg?w=1041" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="NASA Acelerate Laboratory" style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg 1041w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg?resize=768,511 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg?resize=1024,682 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg?resize=400,266 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg?resize=600,399 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/aceleratelab.jpg?resize=900,599 900w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1041px) 100vw, 1041px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">NASA Aerospace Cognitive Engineering Laboratory researchers preparing for a study in the Aerospace Cognitive Engineering Lab Rapid Automation Test (ACELeRATE) simulator.</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>


<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">EMERGENCY RESPONSE OPERATIONS/TRAFFIC MANAGEMENT</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">ACERO</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">Advanced Capabilities for Emergency Response Operations</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">Every year, thousands of wildfires burn roughly 1.5 million acres of land across the United States, causing billions in damages, and incurring around $2.9 billion in annual firefighting costs. Utilizing their expertise in air traffic management, advanced aviation technologies and Unmanned Aircraft Systems (UAS), NASA researchers and scientists are developing and testing new technologies to improve wildland fire coordination and operations for emergency response operators. Human Systems Integration Division researchers, home to the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/integration-training/aol" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/integration-training/aol">Airspace Operations Laboratory (AOL)</a>, are playing a pivotal role in this endeavor.</p>
				<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/armd/aosp/acero-wildfire/" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about ACERO</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="1536" height="1024" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?w=1536" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="NASA scientists watch as a Cal Fire S2-T airtanker drops water on a simulated wildfire, Tuesday, May 4, 2021 as Cal Fire conducts aerial fire fighting training exercises near Redding, California." style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 37% 56%; object-position: 37% 56%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg 5568w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=768,512 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=1024,683 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=1536,1024 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=2048,1365 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=400,267 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=600,400 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=900,600 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=1200,800 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tanker-drop2.jpg?resize=2000,1333 2000w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">NASA scientists watch as a Cal Fire S2-T airtanker drops water on a simulated wildfire, Tuesday, May 4, 2021 as Cal Fire conducts aerial fire fighting training exercises near Redding, California.</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>


<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">CPIDS</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">Cross-Program Integrated Data System</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">The <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/human-computer-interaction/cpids" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/human-computer-interaction/cpids">Cross-Program Integrated Data System (CPIDS)</a> is a collection of digital solutions developed and managed by the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/human-computer-interaction" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/human-computer-interaction">Human-Computer Interaction (HCI)</a> group in the Human Systems Integration Division. The CPIDS project implements common solutions and standard integration mechanisms using cost-effective, well-established technology to share data between disparate computer systems, leverage originating and authoritative sources, and minimize data conversions and process burdens</p>
				<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-computer-interaction/cross-program-integrated-data-system" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about CPIDS</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="1536" height="1012" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?w=1536" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="Screenshot of the Cross-Program Integrated Data System (CPIDS) application, created by the Human System Integration Division&#039;s Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) Group at NASA Ames Research Center." style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 35% 86%; object-position: 35% 86%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png 4340w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=300,198 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=768,506 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=1024,675 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=1536,1012 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=2048,1350 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=400,264 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=600,395 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=900,593 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=1200,791 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/cp-lms-ops-view-on-mbp14.png?resize=2000,1318 2000w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">Screenshot of the Cross-Program Integrated Data System (CPIDS) application, created by the Human System Integration Division&#8217;s Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) Group at NASA Ames Research Center.</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>


<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">AIR TRAFFIC MANAGEMENT</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">ETM</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">Upper Class E Traffic Management</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">Upper-Class E Traffic Management (ETM) is an airspace management concept for <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/nas" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/nas" rel="noopener">National Airspace System (NAS)</a> flight operations at or above FL600. As recent advances in technologies increase the demand in the stratospheric airspace, the diversity of vehicle types and the mixture of operating paradigms present new opportunities for a cooperative and interactive traffic management perspective.<br><br>ETM is part of the NAS Exploratory Concepts and Technologies (NExCT) Sub-Project of the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/aeroresearch/programs/aosp/atm-x/">Air Traffic Management-Exploration</a><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/aeroresearch/programs/aosp/atm-x/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"> (ATM-X)</a> project under the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/aeroresearch/programs/aosp/">Airspace Operations and Safety Program (AOSP)</a> within the <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/aeroresearch">Aeronautics Research Mission Directorate (ARMD)</a>.</p>
				<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/integration-training/airspace-operations-laboratory" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about ETM</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="1536" height="738" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?w=1536" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="Illustration showing the diverse range of high-altitude vehicles that will be studied in the Upper Class E Traffic Management (ETM) Project" style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png 2836w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=300,144 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=768,369 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=1024,492 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=1536,738 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=2048,984 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=400,192 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=600,288 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=900,432 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=1200,576 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/etm-vehicles.png?resize=2000,961 2000w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">Illustration showing the diverse range of high-altitude vehicles that will be studied in the Upper Class E Traffic Management (ETM) Project</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>


<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">HUMAN PERFORMANCE</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">Fatigue- Artemis II</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">Sleep and Wake Countermeasures </p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">In support of <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/mission/artemis-ii/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/mission/artemis-ii/">NASA&#8217;s Artemis II mission</a>, the Fatigue Countermeasures Laboratory at <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/ames/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/ames/">NASA Ames Research Center</a> is helping to maximize the safety performance of Astronauts on this critical mission around the Moon. The aim of the Artemis II Speed and Wake Study was to investigate different interventions and their ability to assist with sleep and alertness for Astronauts during the Artemis lunar human spaceflight missions.</p>
				<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-performance/fatigue-countermeasures-laboratory" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about Fatigue- Artemis II</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="769" height="512" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/nasa-sleeping-in-space-photo-752p-72dpi-courtesy-of-nasa.jpg?w=769" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="Image of an astronaut sleeping in the microgravity of Earth, inside the International Space Station (ISS)." style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 36% 38%; object-position: 36% 38%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/nasa-sleeping-in-space-photo-752p-72dpi-courtesy-of-nasa.jpg 769w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/nasa-sleeping-in-space-photo-752p-72dpi-courtesy-of-nasa.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/nasa-sleeping-in-space-photo-752p-72dpi-courtesy-of-nasa.jpg?resize=400,266 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/nasa-sleeping-in-space-photo-752p-72dpi-courtesy-of-nasa.jpg?resize=600,399 600w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 769px) 100vw, 769px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">Image of an astronaut sleeping in the microgravity of Earth, inside the International Space Station (ISS).</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>


<div style="height:0px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>


<div id="" class="hds-story width-full maxw-full bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-story">
	<div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-y-9 padding-x-4">
		<div class="grid-row">
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-6 desktop:margin-bottom-0">
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h3 class="subtitle-md">HUMAN-COMPUTER INTERACTION</h3>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<h2 class="display-48">Playbook</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="margin-bottom-2">
					<p class="heading-18 line-height-md">Playbook Mission Planning Tool</p>
				</div>
				<p class="p-md">Playbook is a mission planning tool developed by the Division&#8217;s <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/human-computer-interaction" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.nasa.gov/hsi/human-computer-interaction">Human-Computer Interaction (HCI)</a> group. It is a software platform designed to support the next generation of planning, scheduling, and execution tools for space exploration. Timeline scheduling includes ability to model activities, resources, and constraints as well as supports violation checking and resolution. Execution capabilities include procedure integration, tracking status of activities, and multimedia communication. </p>
				<a href="https://www.nasa.gov/human-systems-integration-division/human-computer-interaction/schedule-and-planning-interface-for-exploration" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md">
					<span class="line-height-alt-1">
                        Learn More <span class="usa-sr-only">about Playbook</span>                    </span>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle class="button-primary-circle" cx="16" cy="16" r="16"></circle><path d="M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z" class="color-spacesuit-white"></path></svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 desktop:padding-left-5">
				<div class="width-full display-block">
					<div class="hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto"><figure class="hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover "><img decoding="async" width="1300" height="730" src="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?w=1300" class="attachment-1536x1536 size-1536x1536" alt="NASA astronaut Peggy Whitson is using NASA Playbook, the mobile scheduling tool developed by the Human Systems Integration Division&#039;s Human-Computer Interaction Group, to plan her tasks in the International Space Station (ISS)" style="transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 37% 56%; object-position: 37% 56%; object-fit: cover;" block_context="nasa-block" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg 1300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?resize=300,168 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?resize=768,431 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?resize=1024,575 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?resize=400,225 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?resize=600,337 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?resize=900,505 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/whitson-playbook-autonomous-ops-3.jpg?resize=1200,674 1200w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1300px) 100vw, 1300px" /></figure><figcaption class="hds-caption padding-y-2"><div class="hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0">NASA astronaut Peggy Whitson is using NASA Playbook, the mobile scheduling tool developed by the Human Systems Integration Division&#8217;s Human-Computer Interaction Group, to plan her tasks in the International Space Station (ISS)</div><div class="hds-credits">Credit- NASA</div></figcaption></div>				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	</div>	</div>
</article>

